-- let_forward_reference at 12:13: a `let` VALUE binding is initialised where
-- it is written (`language.md` §6, *Evaluation order*), so `usesLater` may
-- not read `later`, which is bound below it. The name RESOLVES — every
-- binding of a `let` is in scope in every body (§7) — so this is not
-- `unbound_variable`; what it is, is the emitted `const later$2` read inside
-- its own temporal dead zone. Until 2026-09-18 (queue slice 21) this program
-- compiled clean and threw `ReferenceError` under Node. `shown` is legal:
-- `later` is written above it.
f =
    let
        usesLater =
            later + 100

        later =
            5

        shown =
            later + 1
    in
    usesLater + shown
